          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="rte-scenario-result">
        <span class="rte-result-highlight">Federal Tax Owed: $0</span>
        Barbara's Provisional Income of $12,000 is below the $25,000 single-filer threshold, so 0% of her Social Security is taxable. After the 2026 senior standard deduction, her federal tax bill is $0.
      </div>
      <p class="rte-scenario-action"><strong>Outcome:</strong> Barbara stopped worrying about taxes and confirmed she has no filing requirement for the year.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rte-scenario-card">
      <div class="rte-scenario-header">
        <span class="rte-scenario-tag">Scenario 2</span>
        <strong class="rte-scenario-name">James, Age 74 — Social Security + RMD Under the 50% Phase-In Rule</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="rte-scenario-desc">James is single, receives $22,000/year in Social Security, and takes a $15,000 Required Minimum Distribution (RMD) from his Traditional IRA this year.</p>

      <table class="rte-scenario-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Income Source</th>
            <th>Annual Amount</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Social Security</td>
            <td>$22,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="rte-row-alt">
            <td>IRA Required Minimum Distribution (RMD)</td>
            <td>$15,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Provisional Income</td>
            <td>$15,000 + $11,000 (50% SS) = $26,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="rte-row-alt">
            <td>Taxable Social Security (IRC § 86)</td>
            <td>50% of ($26,000 − $25,000 excess) = $500</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Adjusted Gross Income (AGI)</td>
            <td>$15,000 + $500 = $15,500</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="rte-row-alt">
            <td>2026 Senior Standard Deduction (Single 65+)</td>
            <td>$16,100 base + $2,050 senior add-on = $18,150</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="rte-scenario-result">
        <span class="rte-result-highlight">Federal Tax Owed: $0</span>
        Although the $15,000 RMD pushed James’s Provisional Income ($26,000) $1,000 over the $25,000 single threshold, the IRS taxes only 50% of the <em>excess</em> over the threshold ($500), not half of his entire benefit. Because his total AGI of $15,500 remains well below his $18,150 standard deduction, his taxable income is $0 and he owes $0 in federal income tax.
      </div>
      <p class="rte-scenario-action"><strong>Note on Larger Withdrawals:</strong> If James had withdrawn $25,000 instead, his provisional income would reach $36,000 (entering the 85% tier), making $6,200 of Social Security taxable ($31,200 AGI), resulting in $13,050 taxable income and an estimated federal tax bill of $1,318 (or $705 with Schedule 1-A).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rte-scenario-card">
