          </tr>
          <tr class="rte-row-alt">
            <td>Pension (fully taxable)</td>
            <td>$18,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Provisional Income</td>
            <td>$18,000 + $14,000 (50% SS) = $32,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="rte-row-alt">
            <td>Taxable Social Security (IRC § 86)</td>
            <td>$0 (provisional income does not exceed $32,000)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Adjusted Gross Income (AGI)</td>
            <td>$18,000</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="rte-row-alt">
            <td>2026 Senior Standard Deduction (MFJ, Both 65+)</td>
            <td>$32,200 base + 2 × $1,650 senior add-ons = $35,500</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="rte-scenario-result">
        <span class="rte-result-highlight">Federal Tax Owed: $0</span>
        Their Provisional Income of $32,000 hits the exact starting point of the joint-filer 50% threshold. Because there is $0 excess above $32,000, exactly 0% of their Social Security is taxable. With AGI of $18,000 versus their $35,500 married senior standard deduction, their taxable income is $0 and their federal tax is $0.
      </div>
      <p class="rte-scenario-action"><strong>Note on Higher Pension:</strong> If Richard’s pension were $35,000 instead (Provisional Income $49,000), they would cross into the 85% tier with $10,250 taxable Social Security, an AGI of $45,250, and $9,750 taxable income after standard deductions ($975 federal tax, or $0 with Schedule 1-A).</p>
    </div>

    <p class="rte-scenario-disclaimer">These are representative examples based on IRS Publication 915 Provisional Income rules and 2026 IRS standard deduction amounts. Individual results vary based on income mix, filing status, and specific deductions. Verify your result with a tax professional or directly with the IRS before filing.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 6 — COMMON PITFALLS AND HOW TO AVOID THEM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="rte-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Retirement Tax Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Assuming Social Security Is Completely Tax-Free in Retirement</h3>
    <p>This is the single most common retirement tax misunderstanding. Many retirees believe Social Security is tax-exempt, but once Provisional Income crosses $25,000 (single) or $32,000 (joint), up to 85% of Social Security benefits become taxable at ordinary income rates. Because these thresholds have never been inflation-adjusted, the vast majority of Social Security recipients now owe some tax on their benefits.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to calculate your Provisional Income each year before year-end using this free estimator and file Form W-4V to set up withholding on Social Security. Official rules at <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p915" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Publication 915 — Social Security Benefits</a>.</p>

    <h3>Not Planning for the "Tax Torpedo" Created by Large IRA Withdrawals</h3>
    <p>A large IRA withdrawal or RMD increases your Adjusted Gross Income (AGI), which simultaneously increases Provisional Income. This can cause your Social Security to jump from 0% taxable to 85% taxable in the same tax year — meaning the effective federal tax rate on the IRA withdrawal can be much higher than your marginal bracket alone suggests.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to model your full income picture each year before taking large discretionary IRA withdrawals. Use our <a href="/tools/rmd-calculator/">RMD Calculator by Age</a> to understand mandatory withdrawals and our <a href="/tools/roth-conversion-calculator/">Roth Conversion Calculator</a> to evaluate whether converting funds reduces long-term tax exposure.</p>

    <h3>Forgetting to Withhold Tax from IRA and Pension Distributions</h3>
    <p>Unlike wages, IRA distributions and pension payments do not automatically have taxes withheld unless you specifically request it on IRS Form W-4P. Retirees who don't set up withholding often owe a large lump-sum tax payment in April plus potential underpayment penalties.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to file IRS Form W-4P with your IRA custodian or pension administrator immediately. Download Form W-4P at <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/fw4p.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Form W-4P (PDF)</a>.</p>

    <h3>Missing the Additional Standard Deduction for Seniors 65 and Older</h3>
    <p>Many seniors don’t know the IRS provides an additional standard deduction for taxpayers aged 65 and older — $2,050 for single filers and $1,650 per eligible spouse for married couples. This deduction is added on top of the regular 2026 standard deduction ($16,100 single, $32,200 joint) and reduces taxable income directly. Failing to claim it results in overpaying federal taxes.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to check Box 5 on IRS Form 1040 indicating you are 65 or older, which automatically adds the senior deduction. See current amounts at <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-25-32.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS 2026 Tax Inflation Adjustments</a>.</p>
  </section>

